/// Lesson sections for the info page — never shown on the practice screen.
const List<TechniqueSection> setFillLinearLesson = [
  TechniqueSection(
    title: "Why it matters",
    body:
        "A linear fill never plays two things at once: hands and kick take turns, one note per sixteenth. Because the kick fills the gaps, the fill sounds bigger and faster than your hands alone could play it, and it moves around the kit without ever losing the pulse.",
  ),
  TechniqueSection(
    title: "How to play it",
    body:
        "Three bars of rock sixteenths: alternating hi-hat sixteenths with accents on the quarters, snare on 2 and 4, kick on 1, 3 and the \"and\" of 3; bar 1 starts with the crash. In the fill bar line 1 plays R L kick kick on every beat: the hands on the snare for beats 1 and 2, on the high tom for beat 3 and the floor tom for beat 4, the two kicks on the \"and\" and the \"a\". The kick belongs to the fill here, so it does not keep 1 and 3.",
  ),
  TechniqueSection(
    title: "Practice tips",
    body:
        "Say the sticking out loud, \"R L K K\", before you play it. Line 2 is R L R kick walking snare, high tom, mid tom, floor tom; line 3 alternates hand and kick (R K L K). Line 4 groups R L K in threes over four beats and ends with two kicks, so the accent of the group drifts across the beat. Line 7 is an eighth on the tom and two kicks, line 8 the sextuplet R L K twice per beat. Keep the kicks at the volume of the hands; a booming kick breaks the line. Toms accept any tom and the crash any crash, so a kit with fewer drums plays the sheet too.",
  ),
  TechniqueSection(
    title: "Where you hear it",
    body:
        "Gospel and fusion drummers build whole solos from hand-foot groupings; the sextuplet R L K in line 8 is the hands-hands-kick triplet that became a John Bonham trademark.",
  ),
];
